},{"title":"Child Process Node.js v6.2.1-rc.1 Manual & Documentation","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/rc/v6.2.1-rc.1/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/release/v6.8.0/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/release/v8.6.0/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process Node.js v4.0.0-rc.4 Manual & Documentation","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/rc/v4.0.0-rc.4/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/release/v8.2.1/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process Node.js v0.11.13 Manual & Documentation","url":"https://nodejs.org/docs/v0.11.13/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/rc/v6.11.4-rc.2/docs/api/child_process.html"}]},"I've found documentation about Node.js's `child_process.spawnSync()` function. Here's what the documentation shows:\n\n## Overview\n\n`child_process.spawnSync()` provides equivalent functionality to the asynchronous `spawn()` in a synchronous manner that blocks the event loop until the spawned process either exits or is terminated.\n\n## Key Characteristics\n\nThe `node:child_process` module provides synchronous and asynchronous alternatives to `child_process.spawn()` and `child_process.spawnSync()`, with each alternative implemented on top of these core functions.\n\nThe `child_process.spawnSync()` method is **synchronous** and **will** block the Node.js event loop, pausing execution of any additional code until the spawned process exits. Blocking calls like these are mostly useful for simplifying general purpose scripting tasks and for simplifying the loading/processing of application configuration at startup.\n\n## Return Value\n\nThe function returns an object with properties including: pid (the process ID), output (array of results from stdio output), stdout (contents of output[1]), and stderr (contents of output[2]).\n\nFor 